How "Come Back & Finish" Connects to the Future of College
The 2030 Problem It's Solving
There are 36 million Americans with some college and no degree. At Ivy Tech alone, stopped-out students represent the single largest untapped population for completion. The traditional model asks them to start over, make an appointment, wait weeks for an answer, and navigate a system built for 18-year-olds. By 2030, that model loses.

The Three Shifts This Prototype Represents
1. From gatekeeping to self-service clarity
Today a student has to book an advisor just to find out if their credits still count. This tool answers that question in 30 seconds — before they ever make a call. By 2030, every touchpoint in higher ed will need to work this way. Students expect the same instant clarity they get from a bank app or a benefits portal.

2. From one-size-fits-all to life-first scheduling
The default college plan assumes a 19-year-old with no job and no kids. Finding 1 flips that — it shows Jordan a part-time, online, evening path that fits around a warehouse shift. By 2030, the majority of college students will be working adults. Institutions that don't design for that reality won't survive.

3. From degree-or-nothing to stackable credentials
Finding 2 shows Jordan they're 9 credits from a certificate that counts toward the full degree. That's the stackable credential model — earn something real fast, keep going at your own pace. By 2030, Ivy Tech's strategic plan targets this explicitly: shorter credentials that ladder into degrees, so students see value before they're done.

The 2030 Metric It Moves
Stage	Metric	Target
Prototype	Demo viewers who ask a re-enrollment follow-up	> 60%
Pilot Year 1	Stop-out students who begin an application after using the tool	25%
2030	Stop-out students who complete a credential within 24 months of return	1,000 / year
The tool moves the metric because it removes the decision barrier — the moment where Jordan thinks "I don't even know where to start" and closes the tab. When the first screen answers "you're 65% done and 9 credits from a certificate," that moment disappears.

Where This Goes by 2030
This prototype is intentionally small — one student, one program, sample data. But the same engine scales to:

Every Ivy Tech program across all 19 campuses
Real transcript data pulled from the student information system
AI-assisted advising (A-IVY) that surfaces findings like these automatically for every returning student
Proactive outreach — instead of waiting for Jordan to find the tool, the system sends it to every stopped-out student with a text: "You're closer than you think. See your credits →"
That's the 2030 vision: a system that finds Jordan before Jordan has to find it.


